#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192401 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40fb56dd-7e99-5cb6-a3f6-f9ddd630c730" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 2x2 Covariance Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "9f2942ee-4d9f-5436-a021-9e325bfc694b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10450" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n![](images/78/7839eb7b2cfa2511661a6f2822930cd54ef4c3f2cb7df53448af61bdb7f63766.svg)\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $16$ and $6.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\gt \\lambda_2 \\gt \\cdots\\gt \\lambda_n\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_1$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue $\\lambda_1,$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_2$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue $\\lambda_2,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are $\\lambda_1=16$ and $\\lambda_2=6.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=6{:}$\n\nApplying our usual algorithm for finding eigenvectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 15\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-6I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/e5/e5ce684566df94e2d3691bb3fd75b78e46bbfe20bc3ed037618175c619cbe89a.svg) \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2 \\| = \\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n![](images/10/10818d19ffec73c77257977086f97924f8c3ff35fdb4cb46a1a84fa243bbcdbb.svg) \n\nTherefore, the second principal component of the data is ![](images/3c/3cfd9b938a757c8e5b06ebf426b71e0439175e862da98ac7e65e74ee413f2d68.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192623 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192681 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7674fd32-6462-5973-9b40-930637b68c85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 3x3 Covariance Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "1ffdc04e-8224-542d-80f5-99322b693ccf" :question/math-academy-id "e-9836" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n![](images/f5/f51f3460981ca243627f1873a24fa3a50a43275bebdb20c1ced5d64215c71618.svg)\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $S$ are $8,$ $5,$ and $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\gt \\lambda_2 \\gt \\cdots\\gt \\lambda_n\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector $u_1$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue $\\lambda_1,$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector $u_2$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue $\\lambda_2,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=8,\\qquad\n\\lambda_2=5,\\qquad\n\\lambda_3=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=8{:}$\n\nApplying our usual algorithm for finding eigenvectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-8I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-8I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/eb/eb25f50c2550e470fcdffd1c94a5ff2134043ac34b6a75b14e2ce00d1936a2e8.svg) \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| =3,$ we get \n\n![](images/92/920ccfb9953cf4e17f89b01a1f5512f8c0bbcc368f9af1770f768c1e258127e5.svg) \n\nTherefore, the first principal component of the data is ![](images/0c/0c20bbbce9a9014103e94d0f8b055f6dfec18672f2b313d46b1799d9402f755a.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192681 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7674fd32-6462-5973-9b40-930637b68c85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 3x3 Covariance Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1caf9374-a8b9-56f8-9a51-1f6c33377318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513609} {:answer/id #uuid "478cdd6a-a11c-5ded-9658-0ec619567053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513724} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f028a0-351b-53d6-95ad-6d6df0bd24cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514025} {:answer/id #uuid "cf081a75-0c42-57a1-9dee-9c14a25e2410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514073} {:answer/id #uuid "d55415e6-163a-508c-8ecf-e8fbc0a226b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d55415e6-163a-508c-8ecf-e8fbc0a226b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514090} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ebf47f9-948f-5a15-bd99-d2bd7bdca3fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bff16bd8-7b45-5bbd-8528-7bc476ccdc82" :question/math-academy-id "q-137740" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $10,$ $5,$ and $3.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=10,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40fb56dd-7e99-5cb6-a3f6-f9ddd630c730" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 2x2 Covariance Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03883e00-ff01-5a79-b12f-032b887736a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513538} {:answer/id #uuid "058a06a8-4305-5b98-8e52-81f40a72b383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513543} {:answer/id #uuid "1fa9504b-805c-5abe-a81a-22e86efad5cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513615} {:answer/id #uuid "51578d0d-37f6-594d-8ac9-09733a9ba993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513754} {:answer/id #uuid "630b44dc-a913-5dc9-9613-2dda312da9f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fa9504b-805c-5abe-a81a-22e86efad5cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513615} :answer-field/id #uuid "50f8a878-942e-5a59-993e-ff2e07b1ea9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7322fc5-138d-596a-9e83-0b51cc3f5715" :question/math-academy-id "q-138505" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $4$ and $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40fb56dd-7e99-5cb6-a3f6-f9ddd630c730" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 2x2 Covariance Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f6874ad-3005-516e-a0ad-5cb17e1bb218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513569} {:answer/id #uuid "4c15a98b-50e4-52bd-a670-8083176f53dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513740} {:answer/id #uuid "77c2fdcd-3cfd-5138-b031-10ea6cb25796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513848} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ae3c3a-bf66-58f6-85cd-2aa0368d5c16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513967} {:answer/id #uuid "d2934599-3ad3-591c-91b5-c462470674f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77c2fdcd-3cfd-5138-b031-10ea6cb25796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513848} :answer-field/id #uuid "e265595c-4439-5c82-a9e6-7d73e24470a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37adab1d-55b2-5b32-adbd-422291307785" :question/math-academy-id "q-138512" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 11\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $12$ and $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=12$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 11\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192681 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7674fd32-6462-5973-9b40-930637b68c85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 3x3 Covariance Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6efbdee7-7d3a-5707-9520-c57843d2fe06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513832} {:answer/id #uuid "89d016a3-becd-5d35-807d-bea843a21d96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513890} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2c0e02-bfc6-53c8-ba64-3ec942a9b78a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513932} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b50652-4f11-5f47-9388-bfd66be24cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514077} {:answer/id #uuid "dc322190-b60c-5ac0-bb33-83b62a57c1c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc322190-b60c-5ac0-bb33-83b62a57c1c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514110} :answer-field/id #uuid "283a2bc1-2dc2-54df-b080-f5e691fb529a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5ddc6be0-7f0e-552b-88a6-224cbf5ee88c" :question/math-academy-id "q-138521" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $5,$ $4,$ and $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192681 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7674fd32-6462-5973-9b40-930637b68c85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 3x3 Covariance Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f26650b-f307-5083-ac8d-c889e76cf7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 