alues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40fb56dd-7e99-5cb6-a3f6-f9ddd630c730" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 2x2 Covariance Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2146da66-b60d-5beb-a83b-603030a5d8e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513621} {:answer/id #uuid "2b1a9875-89ac-5fc5-9906-3cd3164d6d28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513648} {:answer/id #uuid "592663a7-2308-5850-bedd-0fb5f3ee0ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513771} {:answer/id #uuid "87935c1d-3718-5f26-8460-6ca7631017a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513886} {:answer/id #uuid "b85fd2dd-6759-5b78-